The block distinguishes input components from actual dissolved species and a component ledger from a free-species observation. It develops aliquot/final-volume accounts without silently assuming volume additivity; component and signed charge constraints; the scope of bulk electroneutrality; proton-transfer language; and dimensionless activity/reference conventions before using a logarithmic pH expression. It explicitly separates ideal concentration calculations from operational pH measurement and retains fuller chemical thermodynamics, activity prediction and electrochemistry as unfinished preparation.

For neutral monoprotic HA with only HA, A⁻, H⁺, OH⁻ and water in a homogeneous closed liquid, it derives the four component/charge/acid/water equations and the positive hydrogen residual F(h)=h−K_w,c/h−C K_a,c/(K_a,c+h). Continuity, endpoint limits and a strictly positive derivative prove one admissible root for C≥0 and positive coefficients. Reconstructed species obey component and equilibrium products exactly. A dimensionless finite bracket, bisection width, derivative-based residual certificate and monotonic species bounds establish bounded numerical evidence. This does not prove uniqueness for general multiphase chemistry or validate any empirical coefficients.

The original hypothetical ledger takes analytical stock 9×10⁻⁶ mol/L, aliquot 0.005 L and declared final volume 0.100 L: 4.5×10⁻⁸ mol becomes C=4.5×10⁻⁷ mol/L. With K_a,c=10⁻⁷ mol/L and K_w,c=10⁻¹⁴ (mol/L)², the exact state is h=2×10⁻⁷, OH⁻=5×10⁻⁸, A⁻=1.5×10⁻⁷ and HA=3×10⁻⁷ mol/L. Its ideal pH is the exact expression 7−log₁₀2. No actual preparation, material identity, instrument measurement or laboratory competence is claimed.

The no-water quadratic gives ((√19−1)/2)×10⁻⁷ mol/L, about 16.03% low. The square-root approximation is √(9/8) times the full root, between 6% and 7% high; it happens to be closer because separate errors partially cancel. Both fail a stated 1% hydrogen-concentration criterion. At C=10⁻¹⁰ mol/L with the same coefficients, the full root lies above 10⁻⁷ and below 1.001×10⁻⁷ mol/L, whereas both simplified estimates lie below the neutral point. More arithmetic precision cannot repair omitted water.

A rival with K_a,c'=2×10⁻⁷ mol/L and C'=3×10⁻⁷ mol/L has the same hydrogen, hydroxide and conjugate-base concentrations but HA'=1.5×10⁻⁷ mol/L. Thus a hydrogen/pH observation alone does not determine total and acid strength. A stipulated calibrated concentration-equivalent signal S=HA+2A⁻ distinguishes the cases (6×10⁻⁷ versus 4.5×10⁻⁷); it is a hypothetical response, not a real instrument claim. An independent component assay would address the ambiguity differently.

